\f1\fs22 As Alexander watched his camera team documenting the gurney-based exodus of Haloren Smythe's mortal remains, Kathy attempted to penetrate his directorial trance. She employed the standard protocols for attracting the attention of celebrities: a gentle shoulder tap, followed by a more insistent one, culminating in a desperate glance toward the only being in the known universe with regulatory authority over Alexander Koalemos\'97his mother.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Alexander!\'92 Eloise snapped his name with the same precision required for deploying a tactical nuclear device.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>After a moment, Alexander emerged from his Executive Producer trance. \'91What, mother?\'92 His tone carried the mild irritation of being interrupted while calculating the exact camera angle that would make history remember him as both devastatingly handsome and heroically humble.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Eloise brushed imaginary lint from his suit 'I think the doctor needs to have a word with you.'\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>'Can't it wait? It's almost a wrap.' Alexander used the phrase with the reverence normally reserved for religious incantations or tax exemptions.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>'It's Sarah,' Kathy explained with the practiced patience of someone accustomed to explaining basic human needs to Airborne elites. 'She needs rest.'\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>'Of course,' Alexander replied, deploying the exact facial expression he'd practiced for scenes requiring empathy in his university acting classes. He began patting his pockets with the frantic energy of someone who had misplaced either the nuclear launch codes or their social media password. 'I ah, where is it? Hey, Loosely,' he called to his production assistant, a name the poor fellow had accepted after Alexander had repeatedly failed to remember his actual one. 'Where's the key to the honeymoon suite?'\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy bit her lip with the intensity of someone contemplating which would be more painful: continuing this conversation or performing emergency dental surgery on herself with the nearest butter knife. Eloise, recognising the signs, smoothly intervened.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>'Perhaps it would be better if Sarah came back to Sublime Point,' she suggested, her voice carrying the same gentle insistence that continental drift uses to rearrange geography. 'After such an attack, she's more secure there.'\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>'But, mother.' Alexander's protest carried all the force of a soap bubble in a hurricane.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>'We've spoken about this before, Alexander,' Eloise continued, employing the tone that had intimidated corporate boards, government officials, and particularly stubborn vending machines. 'We agreed she's better off staying at the compound, and you have such important work to attend to.'\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley, who had been monitoring the conversation from the strategically optimal distance for both eavesdropping and plausible deniability, joined the huddle. 'It's exactly as you feared, Lieutenant,' she addressed Alexander, employing a title he had neither earned nor been assigned but had once mentioned wanting after watching a particularly inspiring police procedural. 'I'm sure you already know Haloren Smythe is a deep, undercover\'97'\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander seized Hayley's wrist with the entitlement of someone who believed other people's body parts existed primarily as accessories to his narrative. He pulled her aside to a spot near the elevator where they could speak privately, or at least where he could pretend their conversation wouldn't be immediately reported back to Eloise through whatever surveillance system she had undoubtedly installed in his dental fillings.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>'You're wrong, Holly,' Alexander hissed, mangling Hayley's name with the confidence of someone who believed remembering names was a task best delegated to underlings. 'Smythe is nothing more than a serial-killer bar-fly.'\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>'Okay...' Hayley replied, her tone carrying the same careful neutrality of a zoologist who\'92s unexpectedly found themself in a cage with a faeces-spraying hippo..\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander's eyes lit up with the kind of inspiration that had, throughout history, preceded both groundbreaking inventions and catastrophic mistakes. 'But you're also right. There's a deeper conspiracy we need to expose.' He nodded with slow deliberation. 'Halls of power... out of control agencies...'\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>'Yes,' Hayley agreed. \'92We have our work cut out for us. There's no time to lose.'\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Alexander furrowed his brow in what he believed was thoughtful contemplation. 'I know I should be with Sarah tonight but I think this is just too important, don't you think?'\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>'Oh, I agree,' Hayley offered. 'Sarah will understand.'\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>'Well, of course she will,' Alexander nodded, relief washing over him like the warm glow of knowing he'd successfully avoided an inconvenient obligation. 'I think that doctor wants to run some tests anyway. And I get a bit queasy in a medical environment. All that uncomfortable furniture and bad coffee, intrusive machines and awful smells.' He paused, suddenly noticing the conspicuous absence of those he'd been discussing. 'Hey, where'd they go?'\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley turned to find the space previously occupied by Sarah, Kathy, and Eloise now as empty as Alexander's understanding of marital commitment. She covered her smile with a hand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>'Typical,' Alexander declared indignantly. 'Joan Q. Public has no appreciation of the hours we police professionals need to keep.'\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley nodded enthusiastically, repressing the urge to roll her eyes, until Alexander ruined it with:\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>'I'll be up all night preparing an outline for the script writers to flesh out in the morning. This story isn't going to write itself.'\

